DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The sseemm__ooppeenn() function creates or opens the named semaphore specified by
     _n_a_m_e.  The returned semaphore may be used in subsequent calls to
     sem_getvalue(3), sem_wait(3), sem_trywait(3), sem_post(3), and
     sseemm__cclloossee().

     This implementation places strict requirements on the value of _n_a_m_e: it
     must begin with a slash (‘/’) and contain no other slash characters.

     The following bits may be set in the _o_f_l_a_g argument:

     O_CREAT  Create the semaphore if it does not already exist.

              The third argument to the call to sseemm__ooppeenn() must be of type
              _m_o_d_e___t and specifies the mode for the semaphore.  Only the
              S_IWUSR, S_IWGRP, and S_IWOTH bits are examined; it is not pos‐
              sible to grant only “read” permission on a semaphore.  The mode
              is modified according to the process's file creation mask; see
              umask(2).

              The fourth argument must be an _u_n_s_i_g_n_e_d _i_n_t and specifies the
              initial value for the semaphore, and must be no greater than
              SEM_VALUE_MAX.

     O_EXCL   Create the semaphore if it does not exist.  If the semaphore al‐
              ready exists, sseemm__ooppeenn() will fail.  This flag is ignored unless
              O_CREAT is also specified.

     The sseemm__cclloossee() function closes a named semaphore that was opened by a
     call to sseemm__ooppeenn().

     The sseemm__uunnlliinnkk() function removes the semaphore named _n_a_m_e.  Resources
     allocated to the semaphore are only deallocated when all processes that
     have the semaphore open close it.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     If successful, the sseemm__ooppeenn() function returns the address of the opened
     semaphore.  If the same _n_a_m_e argument is given to multiple calls to
     sseemm__ooppeenn() by the same process without an intervening call to
     sseemm__cclloossee(), the same address is returned each time.  If the semaphore
     cannot be opened, sseemm__ooppeenn() returns SEM_FAILED and the global variable
     _e_r_r_n_o is set to indicate the error.

     The sseemm__cclloossee() and sseemm__uunnlliinnkk() functions return the value 0 if success‐
     ful; otherwise the value -1 is returned and the global variable _e_r_r_n_o is
     set to indicate the error.

EERRRROORRSS
     The sseemm__ooppeenn() function will fail if:

     [EACCES]           The semaphore exists and the permissions specified by
                        _o_f_l_a_g at the time it was created deny access to this
                        process.

     [EACCES]           The semaphore does not exist, but permission to create
                        it is denied.

     [EEXIST]           O_CREAT and O_EXCL are set but the semaphore already
                        exists.

     [EINTR]            The call was interrupted by a signal.

     [EINVAL]           The sseemm__ooppeenn() operation is not supported for the
                        given _n_a_m_e.

     [EINVAL]           The _v_a_l_u_e argument is greater than SEM_VALUE_MAX.

     [ENAMETOOLONG]     The _n_a_m_e argument is too long.

     [ENFILE]           The system limit on semaphores has been reached.

     [ENOENT]           O_CREAT is not set but the named semaphore does not
                        exist.

     [ENOSPC]           There is not enough space to create the semaphore.

     The sseemm__cclloossee() function will fail if:

     [EINVAL]           The _s_e_m argument is not a valid semaphore.

     The sseemm__uunnlliinnkk() function will fail if:

     [EACCES]           Permission is denied to unlink the semaphore.

     [ENAMETOOLONG]     The specified _n_a_m_e is too long.

     [ENOENT]           The named semaphore does not exist.

